# WHO(1)

## NAME

who - who is on the system

## SYNOPSIS

| **who** [ who-file ] [ **am I** ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Who,* without an argument, lists the login name, terminal name, and login time for each
current UNIX user.

Without an argument, *who* examines the /etc/utmp file to obtain its information. If a file is
given, that file is examined. Typically the given file will be /usr/adm/wtmp, which contains a
record of all the logins since it was created. Then *who* lists logins, logouts, and crashes
since the creation of the wtmp file. Each login is listed with user name, terminal name (with
`/dev/` suppressed), and date and time. When an argument is given, logouts produce a similar
line without a user name. Reboots produce a line with `x` in the place of the device name, and
a fossil time indicative of when the system went down.

With two arguments, as in `who am I` (and also `who are you`), *who* tells who you are logged
in as. The terminal it reports is the one on the standard input, reduced to a line name exactly
as login(1) reduced it when it wrote the record, so the two cannot disagree.

**Note:** /usr/adm/wtmp is not kept on this system. Nothing writes it and nothing reads it, so
the paragraph above about logouts, reboots and fossil times describes a file that has to be
made before it describes anything; *who* will read one if it is given one.

**Note:** the name and line fields are eight characters and are *not* terminated when they are
full - see utmp(5) - so a name of exactly eight characters prints as eight characters and no
more. Record 0 of /etc/utmp is always blank: ttyslot(3) counts the lines of /etc/ttys from 1,
and that is the index login(1) writes at. Blank records are suppressed for /etc/utmp and shown
when a file is named, which is what makes a logout visible in a file that keeps them.

## FILES

| /etc/utmp
| /etc/ttys

## SEE ALSO

getuid(2), utmp(5), ttys(5), login(1), mesg(1), write(1)

## DIAGNOSTICS

**Note:** v7 printed `who: cannot open utmp` on the standard output, naming a file it may not
have been given. The diagnostic names the file that failed and goes to the standard error. Exit
status is 1 in that case and 0 otherwise.
